  <Fragment slot="lede">Judge the decision before you judge the outcome. A good call can start with poor results, and a bad one can get lucky.</Fragment>
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>
        Judge the quality of a decision first, then the quality of its outcome.
        Good product decisions share four signs. Principles shape them. Few
        barriers slow them. Input is structured around a small set of risks.
        And the team can tell the right call was made, even when early results
        are modest. Most decisions need about 70% of the information you wish
        you had.
      </p>

      <h2>Why decision quality matters</h2>
      <p>
        As product leaders, one of our most important responsibilities is to
        set clear conditions for good decision making. Decision making is
        complex and challenging, and it's something we all do every single day
        of our careers in product.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also pays. In <a href="https://hbr.org/2010/06/the-decision-driven-organization" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">one study</a>,
        decision effectiveness and financial results correlated at a 95%
        confidence level in companies earning over $1 billion.
      </p>
      <p>
        Many companies think about developer velocity and story points, but
        never focus on decision velocity. Slow decisions are one of the many
        barriers that make a workplace culture stagnant.
      </p>
      <p>
        AI makes this more pressing. Teams track AI feature usage and task
        completion times while missing whether decisions got better. Instead of
        tracking AI feature usage, <a href="/writing/the-case-for-measuring-decision-quality/">measure decision quality</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Sign 1: principles shape decisions</h2>
      <p>
        Alignment on problems without clarity about how to make good decisions
        is where lots of the teams I've worked with have found themselves.
      </p>
      <p>
        When I coach new leaders on stakeholder collaboration, this is often the
        least developed area. They spend a lot of time polishing their ideas, or
        advocating for their point of view, instead of letting several
        tradeoffs sit alongside each other.
      </p>
      <p>
        Few decisions in business are shaped by an obviously good thing and a
        bad thing. <a href="https://medium.com/the-ready/even-overs-the-prioritization-tool-that-brings-your-strategy-to-life-e4f28f2949ac" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Even-over statements</a>
        help. Each one names two good things and says which wins:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Premium quality even over mass market adoption</li>
        <li>Revenue growth even over user growth</li>
        <li>Customer service even over expansion</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The best principles are reversible. A company focused on retention
        might put customer service first. Another might drive retention through
        upsells instead. When a discussion reaches a dead end, or someone digs
        in on one position, bring it back to principles and tradeoffs.
      </p>

      <h2>Sign 2: barriers are few</h2>
      <p>The less important a decision, the faster you should get to it. Common barriers:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Needing "all the data" to make decisions</li>
        <li>Decisions based on influence rather than customer and business needs</li>
        <li>Decisions with long-lasting consequences made without the right stakeholders</li>
        <li>Too many cooks, so decisions end up happening by committee</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Ask your team where they struggle to get to the next step right now. Then do some investigating.</p>

      <h2>Sign 3: input is structured</h2>
      <p>
        I've noticed teams and executives often have a lot of clarity up front
        about what's critical. Over time, risk factors pile up. Soon teams are
        juggling 12 to 15 diverging opinions, risk factors in the dozens and an
        approval process that takes months.
      </p>
      <p>
        What I've come to call structured input fixes this. Stakeholders weigh
        in on what's most important, and the team decides the less critical
        things. <a href="https://www.svpg.com/four-big-risks/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Marty Cagan's four risks</a>
        are a good set: value, usability, feasibility and business viability.
      </p>
      <p>
        The reality is most of the decisions leaders weigh in on are not
        important enough to warrant the meetings they generate. Define the major
        decision milestones up front. Say who supports, who influences and who
        decides. Everyone commits once the call is made. Keep it simple.
      </p>

      <h2>Sign 4: you can judge the call, not just the result</h2>
      <p>
        Decision quality will look different in every organisation. It's made
        of the inputs, the outputs and how decisions flow, for both people and
        machines. One benefit of LLMs is that they force focus on the work
        itself, instead of the work around the work.
      </p>
      <p>Five questions test it:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>What is your context?</li>
        <li>How do you and others know what's important?</li>
        <li>How do you and your teams act on it?</li>
        <li>How do you measure success?</li>
        <li>How do you know the right decision was made, even if the results weren't great to start?</li>
      </ol>
      <p>If a team can answer the last question, it is judging decisions, not just outcomes.</p>

      <h2>When to bring someone in</h2>
      <p>
        If decisions keep stalling at the same barriers, or nobody senior owns
        how product decisions get made, that is a product leadership gap. A
        <a href="/fractional/">fractional product leader</a> can set the
        principles, the decision milestones and the measures, then hand them to
        the team.
      </p>
